    topicSlug: 'web-design',
    readTime: '22 min read',
    intro: `Web design is one of the most misunderstood disciplines in the creative technology world. People assume it is about making things look attractive, about choosing nice colors and picking fonts that seem modern. That assumption is wrong, and it is why so many people who learn web design produce work that looks polished in isolation but fails completely when real users interact with it. Web design is a communication discipline. It is the practice of organizing visual information on a screen in a way that allows a specific audience to understand a specific message and take a specific action as efficiently and comfortably as possible. The aesthetic dimension of web design — the beauty — is a byproduct of solving these communication problems well, not a starting point. When a website is genuinely well-designed, it looks good because it works. This guide builds the complete mental model you need to understand web design as a professional practice, covering the foundational principles that separate lasting skill from temporary trend-chasing.`,
