        heading: 'Designing for Real Users: Research and Empathy',
        body: `The central challenge in web design is that designers are not their users. This is not a problem of skill or intelligence. It is a perceptual problem: designers who work on a product every day know it so thoroughly that they cannot accurately predict how a new user, encountering it for the first time, will experience it. The knowledge that creates expertise also creates blindness to the confusion that novices experience. Resolving this blindness requires systematic research.

User research is the practice of gathering empirical data about how real users think, behave, and experience your product. The most accessible form of user research is observational usability testing: watching real users attempt to complete specific tasks on your website without assistance. The key word is watching. You are not helping, not explaining, not correcting. You are observing. The moments where users hesitate, where they click the wrong thing, where they express confusion or frustration — these moments are data about where your design fails.

Five users completing a usability test will reveal the vast majority of significant usability problems. This is not because five people is a statistically representative sample — it is not. It is because usability problems that affect a meaningful percentage of users almost always surface within the first few test participants. Adding more participants reveals diminishing returns after five to eight. The practical implication is that usability testing does not require a large research operation or formal laboratory setup. Five users, informal tasks, and genuine observation produce actionable insights.

User personas are composite profiles that represent distinct types of users based on research. A persona describes not just demographics but behaviors, goals, frustrations, and contexts of use. "Meera, 32, small business owner, accesses the site primarily from her phone between client meetings, needs to understand pricing in under 30 seconds or she moves on." Personas give design decisions a concrete human referent beyond the designer's own preferences. When choosing between two design directions, asking "which serves Meera better?" produces more focused decisions than debating aesthetic preferences.

Jobs to Be Done is a framework that approaches user needs from a functional perspective. The premise is that users do not really want your product. They want to accomplish a specific goal, and they are "hiring" your product to do that job for them. When someone uses a scheduling tool, the job to be done is not "use a scheduling tool" — it is "never miss a meeting again" or "spend less time on administrative back-and-forth." Understanding the actual job being hired helps designers focus on what truly matters to users rather than on features that seem useful in the abstract.

Accessibility is not a separate consideration from user empathy — it is an expression of it. Approximately 15% of the world's population has some form of disability. Web accessibility means designing and building sites that work for users with visual impairments (who may use screen readers), motor impairments (who may navigate by keyboard rather than mouse), cognitive differences (who may need simpler language and clearer structure), and hearing impairments (who need captions on video content). The Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) provide specific, testable criteria for accessibility at three levels: A, AA, and AAA. Professional web design targets at minimum WCAG AA compliance, which covers the most significant accessibility requirements without requiring impractical design constraints.`
